@startuml NIST Baseline Resolution
title 1. One dimension, plus an optional privacy add-on

artifact "NIST/NIST_SP-800-53_rev5_catalog.yaml\n(catalog)" as catalog
artifact "NIST/NIST_..._MODERATE-baseline_profile.yaml\n(profile — pick exactly one:\nLOW / MODERATE / HIGH)" as profile
artifact "NIST/NIST_SP-800-53_rev5_PRIVACY-\nbaseline_profile.yaml\n(profile — optional add-on,\nnot mutually exclusive)" as privacy
artifact "combining-profile.yaml\n(profile, imports baseline\n+ privacy if selected)" as combiner
artifact "NIST/...-baseline-resolved-\nprofile_catalog.yaml\n(resolved catalog)" as resolved
artifact "example-component-mongodb.yaml\nMongoDB (service)" as mongodb
artifact "example-component-basic-auth.yaml\ntest component 1 (software)" as basicauth

catalog --> profile : import + tailor\n(select controls,\nset parameters)
catalog ..> privacy : import + tailor\n(optional, if system\nprocesses PII)

profile --> combiner : import #1
privacy ..> combiner : import #2\n(if selected)

combiner --> resolved : merge + modify\n(reconcile conflicts,\nsame mechanism as diagram 2)

resolved --> mongodb : implements\n<b>SC-8, SC-8(1), SA-4(9)</b>
resolved --> basicauth : implements <b>AC-2(3)</b>

note right of profile
  The exclusive dimension: pick
  exactly one of LOW / MODERATE /
  HIGH, based on FIPS 199 impact.
end note

note left of mongodb
  MongoDB cites the MODERATE
  profile directly as its sole
  source — a clean single-
  dimension example, no
  raw-catalog hop.
end note

note right of privacy
  PRIVACY isn't a 4th exclusive
  option — it's an orthogonal,
  optional layer on top of
  whichever baseline was picked,
  used when the system processes
  PII.

  Real overlap with MODERATE: 43
  shared controls, 53 privacy-only
  additions — so combining it in
  still needs an intermediate
  profile, even when only one
  source is actually selected.
end note

@enduml
